{
 "section_summary": "This section closes a confidential 1941 economic estimate of Japan, covering transportation, communications, finance, population, labor, foreign investment, and the relationship between economic weakness and expansionist foreign policy. It then begins Frank S. Williams’s Annual Economic Review of Japan for 1940, which documents recession, mounting debt, intensified state control, shortages, troubled foreign trade, and declining industrial and agricultural production. Particular attention is given to cotton textiles, iron and steel, employment, wages, prices, and living costs.",
