   "about": "Continuation and conclusion of a broad assessment of Japan’s transportation, communications, finances, population, labor, foreign investments, and expansionist economic policy.",
   "summary": "The report surveys civil aviation and communications before examining national budgets, defense spending, taxation, banking, savings, public debt, and the increasingly artificial basis of Japan’s financial stability. It argues that the China war had subordinated finance and industry to military policy, while compelled savings and state controls temporarily concealed inflationary pressures and weakening productivity. Population growth, urbanization, labor conditions, foreign investment, and Japan’s penetration of China and Manchukuo are treated as parts of the same drive for an autarkic Asian bloc. The conclusion stresses Japan’s dependence on imported raw materials and American trade, warning that an embargo could encourage military seizure of resources in Southeast Asia and the Netherlands East Indies.",
