  {
   "title": "Annual Economic Review of Japan, 1940 (Report No. 136)",
   "doc_type": "annual economic review",
   "start_page": 329,
   "end_page": 371,
   "continues_previous": false,
   "date": "1941-02-10",
   "from": "Frank S. Williams, American Embassy, Tokyo",
   "to": "Department of State",
   "about": "Annual review of Japan’s economy in 1940, emphasizing intensified state control, financial strain, industrial contraction, foreign-trade problems, and worsening living conditions.",
   "summary": "Completed on January 25 and mailed on February 10, 1941, the report describes 1940 as a year of economic recession despite the government’s drive for national defense and a Greater East Asia Co-Prosperity Sphere. Its general review attributes falling production and trade to the prolonged war in China, the European war, shortages of labor, power and raw materials, and the defects of the controlled economy. Detailed chapters cover legislation, debt and budgets, bond absorption, taxation, money and securities markets, foreign exchange, industrial output, cotton textiles, iron and steel, employment, wages, and prices. The section ends in the opening portion of the agriculture chapter, where official controls are judged unable to overcome shortages of fertilizer, equipment, labor, and agricultural finance.",
